<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xenophon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 430–c. 354 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xenophon was an Athenian soldier, historian and philosopher of the late fifth and fourth centuries BCE, a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>. He served with the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> hired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-younger">Cyrus the Younger</a> in 401 BCE and led their retreat after Cyrus's death. His account, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabasis">Anabasis</a>, demonstrated that a small Greek force could cross the Persian heartland with impunity, exposing the empire's vulnerability.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xenophon.jpg" alt="Xenophon" width="278" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Xenophon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in which a Persian prince hired Greek mercenaries to contest the throne and died at Cunaxa, was especially damaging in strategic terms: <b>Xenophon</b>'s Anabasis showed that a relatively small Greek force could march through the Persian heartland and escape, exposing the empire's vulnerability to any sufficiently determined adversary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Odrysian rulers also hired Greek soldiers: in 400–399 BCE Seuthes II employed the survivors of <b>Xenophon</b>’s Ten Thousand to subdue tribes near the Propontis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ancient writers from <b>Xenophon</b>, in his Constitution of the Lacedaemonians, to Plato, Aristotle and Plutarch discussed Sparta as a model, or a warning, of a state that shaped its citizens through law and education.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Armenians were known long before their language was written: they are named in the 6th-century BC Behistun Inscription and in <b>Xenophon</b>’s Anabasis of the 4th century BC.</blockquote><a href="/language/armenian">Armenian · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Xenophon</b> favoured a moderate oligarchy, and Polybius made oligarchy the degenerate successor of aristocracy in his cycle of constitutions.</blockquote><a href="/government/oligarchy">Oligarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Xenophon</b>’s Hiero imagined the tyrant of Syracuse confessing his unhappiness to the poet Simonides, and Aristotle’s Politics analysed how tyrannies arose, how they were preserved and why they fell.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Erchia, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Corinth, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.92&amp;year=-381" aria-label="Erchia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5146 799 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Xenophon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5596 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5596 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.92&amp;year=-381">What was here in 381 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…it is not numbers or strength that gives victory in war; but, heaven helping them, to one or other of two combatants it is given to dash with stouter hearts to meet the foe…&#8221;</p><footer>Anabasis, book 3 &#183; tr. H. G. Dakyns, 1890 &#183; his own speech to the officers of the Ten Thousand, as he gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1170/pg1170-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We have had occasion before now to reflect how often democracies have been overthrown by the desire for some other type of government, how often monarchies and oligarchies have been swept away by movements of the people…&#8221;</p><footer>Cyropaedia, book 1 &#183; tr. H. G. Dakyns, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2085/pg2085-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The thing is to get them to turn their thoughts to what they mean to do, instead of to what they are likely to suffer.&#8221;</p><footer>Anabasis, book 3 &#183; tr. H. G. Dakyns, 1890 &#183; his own advice to the officers of the Ten Thousand after their generals were seized, as he gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1170/pg1170-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
